WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:07.440
From armies of dragons to tragic heroes, legions&nbsp;
of balrogs, massive wars, foul treachery, and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:12.720
trio of superpowered gems that make the One Ring&nbsp;
feel like your great-grandmother's pearl necklace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:16.560
here are the epic events that shaped&nbsp;
the First Age of Tolkien's Middle-earth.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.240
The First Age comes on the heels of the Years&nbsp;
of the Lamps and the Years of the Trees.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.920
The Years of the Lamps lasts for&nbsp;
millennia and finds the Valar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:30.960
the spirit guardians of Middle-earth,&nbsp;
squaring off against the Dark Lord Morgoth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:34.560
whose second in command, Sauron,&nbsp;
will be the scourge of the Third Age.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:40.640
This world-shaking conflict leads to the Years of&nbsp;
the Trees, which starts after the Valar withdraw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:46.400
to the Blessed Realm. Here two mighty glowing&nbsp;
trees take turns illuminating the continent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.960
In the following millennia, the Elves "awaken"&nbsp;
for the first time. Fearing for the safety of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:56.880
these new creatures, the Valar defeat and capture&nbsp;
Morgoth, and encourage the Elves to come and live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:01.360
with them in the West. Many Elves heed the&nbsp;
call, while others remain in Middle-earth.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:08.160
The next few thousand years are a time of peace as&nbsp;
Morgoth serves a three-age-long prison sentence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.000
and the Elves that went West flourish&nbsp;
in the Blessed Realm. It's at this early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.880
date that both Galadriel and Fëanor are born.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.520
With some help from Shelob's&nbsp;
distant mother, Ungoliant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.760
Morgoth destroys the Two Trees and hightails&nbsp;
it back to Middle-earth where many of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.240
servants — including Sauron — have been hiding.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:31.600
On his way out of the Blessed Realm, Morgoth&nbsp;
kills Fëanor's father and steals the Silmarils.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.480
Artifacts even more sacred&nbsp;
than the light of Eärendil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.560
the Silmarils are jewels that contain&nbsp;
the living light of the destroyed trees.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.760
In the fallout from Morgoth's betrayal,&nbsp;
Fëanor rallies his Elves, known as the Noldor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:49.120
and convinces most of them to disobey the Valar&nbsp;
and leave the Blessed Realm to reclaim the jewels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:54.960
This rebellion is cast in disarray when Fëanor&nbsp;
convinces a loyal subsection of his people to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:00.400
attack other Elves as they leave, seizing their&nbsp;
boats to sail back across the sea to Middle-earth.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.360
As Fëanor sails back to Middle-earth&nbsp;
with a handful of the Noldor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.520
the majority of the Elves are left to&nbsp;
find their own way on foot. This group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.320
which includes Galadriel, didn't participate&nbsp;
in seizing the boats. They decide to head into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:18.800
the frozen north and cross the water at a pass&nbsp;
where the two continents are closer together.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.720
Roughly marking the beginning of the First Age,&nbsp;
the Noldor Elves arrive in Middle-earth. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:29.840
also the moment when the Valar, saddened by the&nbsp;
rebellion of the Elves, still show their support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.440
for them by launching the Sun and the Moon into&nbsp;
the sky to replace the light of the Two Trees.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:40.320
In the eastern regions, Men awaken. While it will&nbsp;
take centuries for them to make their way to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:45.440
Elves, they exist, and they'll eventually&nbsp;
wander right into the First Age action.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:46.960
"Out of the frying pan."

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:50.185
"And into the fire. Run! Run!"

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:56.800
Led by Fëanor, the smaller group of Noldor return&nbsp;
to Middle-earth, catching Morgoth by surprise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.800
The villain has set up shop in&nbsp;
his fortress. Known as Angband,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.640
Morgoth's seat of power is even&nbsp;
more foreboding than Sauron's.

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:10.400
Unprepared for an attack by the Elves, Morgoth's&nbsp;
forces are decimated. Despite Morgoth's resounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:16.400
defeat, Fëanor is mortally wounded by a balrog,&nbsp;
a tough foe for even the mighty Gandalf.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:22.080
After a 75-year pause in the battle, Morgoth&nbsp;
attacks again, but, like Sauron after him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.800
faces defeat at the hands of the Elves.&nbsp;
Emboldened, the Elves attack his mountain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:31.840
fortress. Known as the Siege of Angband,&nbsp;
the battle lasts close to 400 years.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:37.280
As the Siege of Angband drags on, the Elves&nbsp;
that aren't fighting on the front lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.120
establish the hidden cities&nbsp;
of Nargothrond and Gondolin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:45.200
This pair of fortress-kingdoms becomes&nbsp;
crucial in holding off Morgoth.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:49.760
While the primary focus of the First Age&nbsp;
follows the Noldor as they build cities and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:55.280
battle with Morgoth over the Silmarils, another&nbsp;
branch of Elvenkind is also important. Known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:01.920
as the Sindar or the Grey Elves, they're the&nbsp;
Elven race of Thranduil and his son, Legolas.

00:04:02.960 --> 00:04:08.080
Initially, the Elves continue to deal with&nbsp;
occasional attacks from Morgoth's forces. Two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:15.040
hundred years into the siege, the first dragon,&nbsp;
Glaurung, ancestor to Smaug, appears. Still young,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:19.920
it doesn't take much for the Elves to send&nbsp;
him scampering back to his master. After this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:25.600
the "Long Peace" begins, during which Morgoth&nbsp;
remains quiet and the Elves remain vigilant.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:30.560
The Long Peace is a time of great prosperity&nbsp;
and optimism. Believing that the Silmarils&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.480
will soon be back in their hands,&nbsp;
the kingdoms of the Elves thrive.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:40.720
During the Long Peace, Men enter Middle-earth&nbsp;
history. Wandering in from the Eastern lands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:45.920
many of these Men are seduced by Morgoth&nbsp;
just as King Théoden will fall under Sauron's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:51.360
spell in The Lord of the Rings. However, three&nbsp;
clans befriend the Elves and join their cause.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:55.840
These Men are known as the&nbsp;
Edain or "The Three Houses."

00:04:57.522 --> 00:04:59.522
"I am no man!"

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.280
The Long Peace ends when Morgoth unexpectedly&nbsp;
unleashes rivers of fire from his mountain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:11.680
fortress. Leading armies of orcs and balrogs,&nbsp;
a now-fully-grown Glaurung attacks the Elves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.560
Known as the Battle of Sudden Flame,&nbsp;
the assault leaves the Noldor reeling.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.280
While the Battle of Sudden Flame is a solid&nbsp;
defeat for the forces of good, it does lead to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:27.360
two of the most epic events in all of Tolkien's&nbsp;
writings. First, the Noldor King, Fingolfin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:33.360
becomes so enraged that he rides to Angband to&nbsp;
challenge the dark lord Morgoth to single combat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.720
just as Eowyn will one day&nbsp;
face the Witch King alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:42.000
The duel is intense as the Elf-lord dodges&nbsp;
Morgoth's deadly mace and manages to wound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:47.920
him seven times. Despite his valiant efforts,&nbsp;
the brave Fingolfin dies by Morgoth's hand.

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:50.080
Nearly 20 years after their initial defeat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.080 --> 00:05:53.920
the Elves gather their forces&nbsp;
and attack Morgoth in one final,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:59.920
massive bid for victory. One of the most glorious&nbsp;
First Age battles, the Battle of Unnumbered Tears,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:04.400
leaves the Elven armies in ruins and Morgoth&nbsp;
virtually unchallenged in Middle-earth.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:10.000
While the Battle of Unnumbered Tears makes Morgoth&nbsp;
the most powerful being on the continent, that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:16.080
doesn't mean his enemies are completely defeated.&nbsp;
The kingdoms of Nargothrond, Gondolin, and Doriath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:21.680
remain. Well hidden, these kingdoms are reluctant&nbsp;
to resist the resurgent Dark Lord in the open.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:26.480
The Elves never fully recover from the Battle&nbsp;
of Unnumbered Tears. Weak and divided, they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:32.720
slowly consumed by the forces of darkness.&nbsp;
Morgoth discovers Nargothrond and Gondolin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:37.040
the first by stupidity, and the second&nbsp;
by treachery, and destroys them both.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:43.680
"I will not stand down before any Elf! Not&nbsp;
least this faithless, woodland sprite!"

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:49.280
Romance in Tolkien's writing isn't limited&nbsp;
to Aragorn and Arwen. One of the author's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:55.840
most celebrated First Age epics is a love story.&nbsp;
The Tale of Beren and Lúthien begins when Beren,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:00.880
a Man, escapes into the enchanted kingdom&nbsp;
of Doriath after his lands are devastated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:06.720
by the Battle of Sudden Flame. There, he meets&nbsp;
Lúthien, the daughter of the Elf king Thingol,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:10.400
and the two fall in love. However, the&nbsp;
king is appalled at the thought of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:14.480
daughter marrying a fugitive and a&nbsp;
mortal man. He jokingly tells Beren&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:19.120
that if he can personally get one of the&nbsp;
three Silmarils from Morgoth's iron crown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:24.960
an act even more daring than Bilbo's theft&nbsp;
of the Arkenstone, he can marry his daughter.

00:07:24.960 --> 00:07:30.080
Beren departs on his quest, but soon finds that&nbsp;
Lúthien has come to help him. Side by side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:33.440
the lovers travel to Angband and steal a Silmaril.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:38.160
Throughout the adventure, Lúthien pulls most&nbsp;
of the weight with her magical abilities.

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:43.440
As they return home, the great wolf Carcharoth,&nbsp;
who makes The Hobbit's wargs look like mere pups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:49.040
steals the Silmaril, biting off Beren's hand in&nbsp;
the process. Eventually, they hunt Carcharoth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:56.480
down and recover the jewel, but Beren dies of his&nbsp;
wounds. Grief-stricken, Lúthien also perishes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:02.160
Their tragic romance is so moving that the couple&nbsp;
is returned to life. Lúthien is made mortal and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.320
the pair are allowed to return to Middle-earth,&nbsp;
where they live for a while longer together.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:12.800
An epic tragedy, the tale of The Children of&nbsp;
Húrin is the story of a brave chieftain of Men&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:17.520
captured during the Battle of Unnumbered&nbsp;
Tears. Morgoth, in an act more cruel than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:21.840
even anything conceived by The Lord of the&nbsp;
Ring's Witch King, tortures Húrin hoping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:27.600
he will reveal the location of the hidden city&nbsp;
of Gondolin. Húrin is unyielding. Angered, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:33.040
Dark Lord curses Húrin's family and forces him to&nbsp;
witness the destruction of his wife and children.

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:38.800
Húrin's son, Túrin, is at first raised by&nbsp;
the Elves in Doriath, but he flees after he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:44.640
accidentally kills a mocking Elf in a brawl. From&nbsp;
there, he launches into a heroic career to rival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:50.160
that of the Third Age's Boromir. Repeatedly,&nbsp;
he rises to glory only to face utter ruin.

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:55.200
Túrin heads to the hidden kingdom of Nargothrond,&nbsp;
where he convinces the Elvish leadership to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:59.760
fight in the open, leading to the utter&nbsp;
destruction of the cavernous underground realm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:04.240
Unfortunately, Glaurung the Dragon,&nbsp;
deceitful as The Hobbit's Smaug,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:08.720
brainwashes both Túrin and his sister&nbsp;
Níniel. Under the dragon's enchantment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:14.080
the two meet and, not knowing they're related,&nbsp;
marry, and conceive a child. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:18.800
Túrin kills Glaurung. Realizing what they've&nbsp;
done, the siblings take their own lives.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:24.000
The third great epic of the First&nbsp;
Age is the tale of Eärendil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:29.280
the Mariner. Distantly related to all three&nbsp;
of the Houses of Men as well as the Elves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:33.520
Eärendil is born in Gondolin and flees&nbsp;
the city's destruction as a child.

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:38.480
Escaping to the western shores of Middle-earth,&nbsp;
where a group of exiles has settled in the wake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:45.200
of Morgoth's resurgent power, Eärendil marries&nbsp;
Elwing, the granddaughter of Beren and Lúthien&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:50.800
who herself will be the grandmother of Gondor's&nbsp;
future queen Arwen. The parents of Elrond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:55.520
and his brother Elros, who begins the&nbsp;
line of kings that leads to Aragorn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:58.880
Eärendil and Elwing are a Tolkien power couple.

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:04.000
Eärendil becomes famous in his own right when&nbsp;
he builds a ship and heads out in search of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:10.000
the Blessed Realm. Eventually, Elwing joins him.&nbsp;
Together, they reach the Blessed Realm and ask the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:14.480
Valar to return to Middle-earth and help their&nbsp;
relatives, who are on the brink of destruction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:19.120
The guardians, moved by the personal sacrifice,&nbsp;
forgive the Elves and respond to the call.

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:25.520
Once the Valar receive Eärendil's plea for&nbsp;
aid, they gather a massive army and head to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:30.480
Middle-earth to face down Morgoth once and for&nbsp;
all. Morgoth has grown even more powerful, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:35.840
unlike his lieutenant Sauron, doesn't need a magic&nbsp;
ring to inflict his will. Known as the War of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:41.120
Wrath, the conflict that ensues makes the Battle&nbsp;
of the Five Armies look like a barroom brawl.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:46.880
Elves, Men, gods, eagles, dragons,&nbsp;
balrogs, and orcs enter the fray in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:50.960
all-out battle of good versus evil.
 
Morgoth's armies are powerful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:55.040
but ultimately, they can't overcome&nbsp;
the host from the Blessed Realm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.040 --> 00:11:00.880
Facing a defeat as total as Sauron's future fall,&nbsp;
Morgoth is dethroned and his armies vanquished.

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:06.080
However, the fight is so intense that it breaks&nbsp;
the land, causing a flood. As water rushes in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:11.360
the region is completely submerged. This event&nbsp;
creates the familiar map of Middle-earth we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:16.720
from The Lord of the Rings. With the land sinking&nbsp;
beneath their feet, most of the Elves and Valar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:21.440
head back to the Blessed Realm. However, many&nbsp;
others, both good and evil, choose to head&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:27.040
east. Here, the First Age ends and the Second&nbsp;
Age begins, and with it comes a new threat in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:32.240
the form of Morgoth's second in command, Sauron,&nbsp;
who will prove to be an even more powerful foe.

